
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {

        // Step 1: Convert both strings into character arrays
        // because Arrays.sort() works on arrays, not directly on String
        char[] arr1 = s.toCharArray();
        char[] arr2 = t.toCharArray();

        // Step 2: Sort both character arrays
        // If two strings are anagrams, their sorted forms will be identical
        Arrays.sort(arr1);
        Arrays.sort(arr2);

        // Step 3: Convert sorted character arrays back to Strings
        // so we can easily compare them using .equals()
        String sorted1 = new String(arr1);
        String sorted2 = new String(arr2);

        // Step 4: Compare the sorted strings
        // If they are equal, then both original strings are anagrams
        if (sorted1.equals(sorted2)) {
            return true;   // strings are anagrams
        } else {
            return false;  // strings are NOT anagrams
        }
    }
}

⏱️ Time and Space Complexity of Your Approach
🔹 Time Complexity: O(n log n)

Converting strings to arrays → O(n)

Sorting both arrays → O(n log n) each

Comparing strings → O(n)

👉 Overall:

O(n log n)

(Sorting dominates the time)

🔹 Space Complexity: O(n)

You create:

2 char arrays → arr1, arr2

2 new strings → sorted1, sorted2

👉 Total extra space:

O(n)
